SIGNAL INTEGRITY IN HIGH-SPEED PCB DESIGN
Part 1 fundamentals  |  source: layout practice notes

WHY IT BREAKS AT SPEED
At low rates, traces look like simple wires.
As edge rates get faster, traces become transmission lines.
The critical variable is rise/fall time (tr/tf), not just data rate.
When electrical length approaches the edge, reflections and coupling dominate.

COMMON FAILURE MODES
  ringing              overshoot / undershoot from unterminated energy
  reflections          impedance discontinuities (Z mismatch, vias, corners)
  crosstalk            mutual capacitance / inductance between neighbors
  ground bounce        return-path inductance under fast di/dt
  impedance mismatch   Zsource != Z0 != Zload

KEY VARIABLES
  Z0     characteristic impedance of the trace
  RT     termination resistance  (goal: RT ≈ Z0)
  tr/tf  edge rate  (sets bandwidth and when a trace is "long")
  l      trace length
  Cmut   mutual capacitance
  Lmut   mutual inductance
  typical single-ended Z0 ≈ 50 Ω
  typical differential Zdiff ≈ 100 Ω

1. IMPEDANCE CONTROL
Match trace Z0 and absorb leftover energy at the ends.

  Parallel
    RT = Z0 at the load
    simple, burns DC current

  Thevenin
    R1 to VCC, R2 to GND
    R1 || R2 = Z0
    lower DC current than single parallel RT

  Active parallel
    RT = Z0 into a bias source VBIAS
    driver can source/sink against a defined bias

  Series-RC
    series R + C at the load
    blocks DC, damps high-frequency components

  Series (source)
    Rs ≈ Z0 - Zdriver at the source
    kills secondary reflections; slower edge at far end

  Differential pair
    RT across the pair at the receiver
    RT ≈ Zdiff ≈ 100 Ω
    each line often ~50 Ω to ground

No universal winner. Pick by power budget, topology, and timing.

2. TRACE GEOMETRY AND VIAS
90° corners and sloppy vias create Z discontinuities and extra radiation.
Use two 45° bends or a smooth curve.
Keep the miter / diagonal segment longer than ~3W.
Place vias with continuous return path; avoid long stubs.

3. COUPLING-AWARE ROUTING
Signals interact. Route the pair, not just the net.
  orthogonal routing on adjacent layers
  keep parallel runs short  (rule of thumb: avoid long side-by-side >500 mil)
  cut driver fan-out and extra loads
  give every fast edge a nearby return path

REMEMBER
Data rate is a symptom. Edge rate sets the frequency content.
Match Z0, terminate the unused energy, and keep geometry continuous.
